WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:04.560
Makeup artists are often the unsung heroes
of horror movies.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.010
From classic monsters to modern terrors, you
won't believe what these creepy characters

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:11.312
look like behind their masks.

00:00:11.312 --> 00:00:16.750
Although writer Clive Barker didn't name the
lead Cenobite "Pinhead" in his novel "The

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:21.019
Hellbound Heart," the character's distinctive
appearance earned the character the nickname

00:00:21.019 --> 00:00:23.410
that's stuck throughout the "Hellraiser" series.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:29.550
Ever since Barker adapted his own story to
the screen in 1987, English actor Doug Bradley

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.803
has become synonymous with the soul-harvesting
Pinhead.

00:00:32.803 --> 00:00:38.803
"We have such sights to show you."

00:00:39.063 --> 00:00:43.940
Bradley is part of a special group of horror
actors who have played their haunting characters

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:46.140
at least six times in a row.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:51.820
He's accompanied by Tobin Bell as Jigsaw,
Warwick Davis as the Leprechaun, Brad Dourif

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:56.559
as Chucky, Christopher Lee as Dracula, and
Robert Englund as Freddy Krueger — but he's

00:00:56.559 --> 00:01:01.090
also developed a somewhat complicated relationship
with the franchise.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:06.990
Bradley opted not to return for 2011's "Hellraiser:
Revelations," and the saga seems to have moved

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:08.410
on without him.

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:13.700
2018's "Hellraiser: Judgment" marked the second
installment to feature a different actor under

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:20.100
the makeup, and 2022's "Hellraiser" reboot
changed things up by casting Pinhead as a woman.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.799
Still, Bradley will always be Pinhead to us,
and we have to think that the majority of

00:01:23.799 --> 00:01:25.500
franchise fans agree.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:31.870
Tobe Hooper's 1974 horror flick "The Texas
Chainsaw Massacre" spawned several kooky and

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:36.170
bizarre sequels, but the original is notable
for how much it stood out from other genre

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:37.650
entries from the era.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:42.649
Heavily inspired by the horrific true story
of Ed Gein, Leatherface got his name from

00:01:42.649 --> 00:01:45.790
his revolting headpiece made of human skin.

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:49.810
While plenty of people have picked up the
chainsaw since, we'll always remember Gunnar

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:53.650
Hansen for his original — and only — portrayal
of the role.

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:58.830
Hansen set the standard for how to properly
play a chainsaw-wielding maniac, utilizing

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:03.969
mannerisms that have been repeated time and
time again in the character's other appearances.

00:02:03.969 --> 00:02:08.770
Fans of the B movie "Mosquito" will also recognize
Hansen as one of its main characters — who

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:13.550
actually uses a chainsaw to fight off enormous
blood-sucking bugs.

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:18.819
Hansen turned down a number of later opportunities
to play terrifying bad guys, opting instead

00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:21.069
to focus on his passion for writing.

00:02:21.069 --> 00:02:25.300
Eventually, Hansen returned to Hollywood,
accepting roles in horror outings like "Swarm

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:26.300
of the Snakehead."

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:32.319
Sadly, though, Hansen succumbed to pancreatic
cancer in 2015 at the age of 68.

00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:37.080
Ridley Scott's "Alien" revolutionized both
the science fiction and horror movie genres

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.629
in 1979.

00:02:38.629 --> 00:02:42.950
When pre-production began, no one knew who
was going to be cast as the film's titular

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:49.709
monster — but eventually, Ridley Scott was
introduced to Nigerian design student Bolaji Badejo.

00:02:49.709 --> 00:02:54.909
Badejo's lanky frame and near-seven-foot-tall
height would help convince viewers that the

00:02:54.909 --> 00:02:59.140
film's monster was not just a guy in a suit,
but an extraterrestrial killing machine.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:05.549
"You still don't understand what you're dealing
with, do you?

00:03:05.549 --> 00:03:07.739
Perfect organism."

00:03:07.739 --> 00:03:12.560
Badejo only played the alien in the first
film of the franchise, but still left a heavy

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:13.560
impression.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:18.069
Future "Alien" installments used puppetry
and CGI to render their aliens, making Badejo's

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:21.709
Xenomorph a curious, yet uniquely threatening
oddity.

00:03:21.709 --> 00:03:25.990
Badejo ended up doing an amazing job to help
sell the illusion, and the Xenomorph would

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:30.760
go on to become one of Hollywood's most fearsome
and unforgettable sci-fi monsters.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:35.740
Sadly, Badejo's "Alien" legacy would long
outlive him — he was only 39 when he passed

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:38.930
away from sickle cell anemia in 1992.

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:44.939
Originally, Jean-Claude Van Damme was cast
to play the Predator in the 1987 action flick

00:03:44.939 --> 00:03:46.269
of the same name.

00:03:46.269 --> 00:03:50.439
Unfortunately, the bulked-out appearance of
star Arnold Schwarzenegger and the rest of

00:03:50.439 --> 00:03:55.409
the cast made Van Damme seem way too small
for the role — despite how amazing it would

00:03:55.409 --> 00:03:57.959
have been to see his Predator's kung fu skills.

00:03:57.959 --> 00:04:03.049
Van Damme was scrapped in favor of Kevin Peter
Hall, who also starred as the towering title

00:04:03.049 --> 00:04:05.799
character in "Harry and the Hendersons" the
same year.

00:04:05.799 --> 00:04:10.489
When you've got guys like Jesse "The Body"
Ventura and Carl Weathers walking around a

00:04:10.489 --> 00:04:14.519
jungle, you're going to want to make your
movie monster a true behemoth, and not the

00:04:14.519 --> 00:04:16.820
five-foot-ten muscles from Brussels.

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:22.560
Thankfully, Hall's tall frame made for a perfect
Predator in both the 1987 original and its

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.360
1990 sequel, "Predator 2."

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:29.270
The year after the sequel's release, Hall
passed away at the tragically young age of

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:34.419
35, his life and career cut short by AIDS-related
pneumonia.

00:04:34.419 --> 00:04:40.880
John Carpenter's low-budget 1978 slasher classic
"Halloween" helped pave the way for a generation

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:46.479
of unforgettable killers with the film's lumbering
silent psychotic villain— and future franchise

00:04:46.479 --> 00:04:48.750
star — Michael Myers.

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:53.530
Carpenter added a heavy psychological aspect
to the killer that we hadn't seen since Norman

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:59.220
Bates decades prior, and envisioned the film's
location — fictional Haddonfield, Illinois

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:05.222
— as a typically idyllic suburb that turned
unspeakably creepy after sunset on October 31.

00:05:05.222 --> 00:05:09.910
After donning the mask in the first movie,
Nick Castle's performance as The Shape would

00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:11.842
be imitated for years to come.

00:05:11.842 --> 00:05:17.140
He went on to enjoy a varied career, too — he
co-wrote Carpenter's dystopian action flick

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:21.710
"Escape from New York," helped write the story
for Steven Spielberg's "Hook," and has directed

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:26.020
an assortment of movies that includes "The
Last Starfighter" and "The Boy Who Could Fly."

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:31.490
Forty years after making horror history, Castle
came full circle, agreeing to reprise his

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:35.980
role as Michael Myers in the 2018 "Halloween"
sequel.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:40.410
How could we assemble a list like this without
including the man of our dreams?

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:45.479
As much as Jackie Earle Haley chewed the scenery
in the 2010 remake of "A Nightmare on Elm

00:05:45.479 --> 00:05:51.440
Street," only one actor's name truly resonates
with horror movie nightmares: Robert Englund.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:56.319
Horror fans got to watch Englund wield the
bladed glove for nearly two decades, as he

00:05:56.319 --> 00:06:00.020
embodied the twisted character of Freddy Krueger
over the course of eight films.

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:04.850
He even got his own anthology horror series
— the aptly named "Freddy's Nightmares,"

00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:06.460
which ran for two seasons.

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:11.009
There's no denying that Freddy is one of the
most recognizable horror icons out there.

00:06:11.009 --> 00:06:16.420
Sure, his antics became a lot sillier as the
years went by, but "Wes Craven's New Nightmare"

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:19.660
was a proper return to form for the supernatural
slasher.

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:24.830
Maybe someday, Englund will don the fedora,
sweater, and bladed glove one last time for

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:26.698
a final trip down Elm Street.

00:06:26.698 --> 00:06:29.718
Until then, we can only dream about it.

00:06:31.671 --> 00:06:35.048
"Miss me?"

00:06:35.048 --> 00:06:39.060
Yes, it's true: lots of people have played
Jason Voorhees.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:43.360
We've seen the character go through some dramatic
changes over the years: he wasn't even the

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:48.199
main killer in the first "Friday the 13th"
movie, and he didn't start wearing his iconic

00:06:48.199 --> 00:06:49.940
hockey mask until the third.

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:54.360
A whopping ten actors have played the character
so far, but we'd have to choose Kane Hodder

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.300
as the definitive Jason.

00:06:56.300 --> 00:07:02.220
While he didn't even sign onto the series
until "Friday the 13th Part VII: The New Blood,"

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:06.949
Hodder's role helped bring Jason into a new
age of slasher flicks and made him the hulking,

00:07:06.949 --> 00:07:09.540
unstoppable menace that we all love and remember.

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:14.699
He's also had the most experience playing
Jason, as nearly every movie prior to "Part

00:07:14.699 --> 00:07:17.170
VII" had someone different in the part.

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:21.479
Donning the mask for four films — "The New
Blood," "Jason Takes Manhattan," "Jason Goes

00:07:21.479 --> 00:07:27.020
to Hell: The Final Friday," and "Jason X"
— Hodder remains a horror heavyweight champion

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:28.129
in our books.

00:07:28.129 --> 00:07:32.410
Hodder isn't done with Jason yet, either:
he did motion capture work for "Friday the

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:36.980
13th: The Game," offering fans a chance to
be part of the action for a whole new series

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:38.560
of thrills.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:43.449
If you've seen a film by director James Wan,
chances are you've probably spied composer

00:07:43.449 --> 00:07:46.259
Joseph Bishara's name amongst the credits.

00:07:46.259 --> 00:07:52.480
His scores appear in "Insidious," "The Conjuring,"
"Insidious: Chapter 2," "The Conjuring 2,"

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:54.180
"Aquaman," and "Malignant."

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:59.229
He's also provided musical stylings for other
films in The Conjuring universe — from "Annabelle"

00:07:59.229 --> 00:08:00.659
to "The Curse of La Llorona."

00:08:00.659 --> 00:08:06.250
But look closely, and you'll notice that Bishara
enjoys more than just a composer credit in

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:07.840
a slew of these films.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:13.220
Yes, the demonic beasties in Wan's films — and
the larger Conjuring universe — are portrayed

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:16.289
by the same guy who wrote their killer soundtracks.

00:08:16.289 --> 00:08:21.089
You can spy Bishara in the quick glimpses
of the witch Bathsheba in "The Conjuring"

00:08:21.089 --> 00:08:26.410
and as Malthus, the demonic entity possessing
the titular doll in the "Annabelle" films.

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:32.190
However, Bishara's portrayal of the Lipstick-Face
Demon in the "Insidious" films is his greatest

00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:34.760
on-screen contribution to horror history.

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:38.959
Maybe it's because he looks like Darth Maul's
cousin — or maybe it's his love of Tiny

00:08:38.959 --> 00:08:44.970
Tim — but this child-killing demon is undeniably
compelling thanks to Bishara's intimidating

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:47.250
screen presence.

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:51.860
It's hard to pinpoint the best part of "The
Return of the Living Dead," the hilariously

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:57.130
bleak 1985 zombie horror film directed by
Dan O'Bannon — but in a film full of nothing

00:08:57.130 --> 00:09:01.890
but highlights, one goopy lad steals the show:
Tarman.

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:08.270
After the character breaks out of a military-grade
metal drum, he stumbles, sloshes, and drips

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:13.443
his way from the basement to the ground floor
of a medical supply warehouse.

00:09:15.448 --> 00:09:17.888
"Brains!"

00:09:18.409 --> 00:09:20.406
"BRAINS!"

00:09:21.031 --> 00:09:26.380
While some close-up scenes used a puppet,
the longer shots required an actor.

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:31.279
That actor ended up being performer and puppeteer
Allan Trautman, who some may know best for

00:09:31.279 --> 00:09:35.339
his work on Jim Henson projects like "The
Muppets" and "Dinosaurs."

00:09:35.339 --> 00:09:40.480
Trautman is still bringing creatures to life,
having lent his voice and puppeteering talents

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:45.070
to 2016's "The Jungle Book" and 2021's "Muppets
Haunted Mansion."

00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:50.209
Given that Trautman reprised his role as the
goopy ghoul in "Return of the Living Dead

00:09:50.209 --> 00:09:55.220
Part II," we're going to assume that, like
us, the actor has a soft spot for this rotting

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:58.260
hunk of reanimated flesh.

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:03.459
Taking up the role of an already iconic horror
movie monster can be a daunting task — one

00:10:03.459 --> 00:10:06.380
that even the best actors aren't always up
to.

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:12.350
Given how excellent Tim Curry is in the 1990
TV movie original, whoever stepped in as Pennywise

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:17.950
for the big-screen 2017 version of "It" was
going to have some massive red clown shoes

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:18.846
to fill.

00:10:18.846 --> 00:10:21.675
Thankfully, Bill Skarsgård was up to the
task.

00:10:21.675 --> 00:10:26.600
His performance differs greatly from Curry's
— a smart choice, since it's typically best

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:29.709
to make something your own rather than imitate
the original.

00:10:29.709 --> 00:10:35.149
He's uniquely terrifying as Pennywise, more
overtly sinister and inhuman than Curry's

00:10:35.149 --> 00:10:39.412
frightening but undeniably carnival barker-esque
turn.

00:10:39.412 --> 00:10:41.610
"Come join the clown, Eds.

00:10:42.027 --> 00:10:44.457
You'll float down here.

00:10:44.457 --> 00:10:46.871
We all float down here."

00:10:46.871 --> 00:10:51.519
Skarsgård comes from a family of Swedish
actors: his brother Alexander and father Stellan

00:10:51.519 --> 00:10:52.790
are also well known.

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:57.090
After appearing in a number of Swedish films,
he found his break into the American market

00:10:57.090 --> 00:11:03.089
as Roman Godfrey on the Netflix original program
"Hemlock Grove" for three seasons.

00:11:03.089 --> 00:11:08.779
Since the show wrapped in 2015, he's also
made appearances in "Atomic Blonde," "Divergent:

00:11:08.779 --> 00:11:11.070
Allegiant," and "Assassination Nation."

00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:16.339
As far as horror goes, he also played a featured
role in "Barbarian" and starred in the Stephen

00:11:16.339 --> 00:11:20.690
King-inspired Hulu original series "Castle
Rock."

00:11:20.690 --> 00:11:24.639
If you're going into "Barbarian" for the first
time, you might think that the bad guy is

00:11:24.639 --> 00:11:29.260
going to be Keith, the suspicious fellow played
by Bill Skarsgård — but writer-director

00:11:29.260 --> 00:11:32.720
Zach Cregger has a few curveballs in store.

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:37.519
After the secret beneath the barbaric AirBnB
is revealed, Keith falls victim to the film's

00:11:37.519 --> 00:11:43.019
true villain: "The Mother," a large, naked,
aggressive woman who lives in the tunnel system

00:11:43.019 --> 00:11:44.390
below the house.

00:11:44.390 --> 00:11:49.610
Matthew Patrick Davis, a 6'8" theater artist
with a sketch comedy background, portrays

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:51.430
The Mother in all her glory.

00:11:51.430 --> 00:11:57.070
Davis' on-screen work has been predominantly
on TV shows and short films, making "Barbarian"

00:11:57.070 --> 00:11:58.980
an interesting change of pace.

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:03.959
In a chat with No Film School, Davis revealed
that he booked the "Barbarian" gig by sending

00:12:03.959 --> 00:12:10.209
in a self-tape of himself half-naked "baby-birding"
a pickle into an imaginary victim's mouth.

00:12:10.209 --> 00:12:13.662
When asked about the most challenging part
of his role, he responded —

00:12:13.662 --> 00:12:17.310
"I think the night shoots, because I mean,
I was ass out.

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:19.508
I was nearly nude, and so it was cold."

00:12:19.508 --> 00:12:24.449
Davis' efforts paid off, though, as his performance
helped make "Barbarian" one of the biggest

00:12:24.449 --> 00:12:27.170
surprises of 2022.

00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:31.529
Excuse us for being nostalgic, but we get
a little wistful when we think back to the

00:12:31.529 --> 00:12:34.850
days when superhero movies could flirt with
the horror genre.

00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:40.590
Case in point: 1998's "Blade," the first feature
film that sees Wesley Snipes' day-walking,

00:12:40.590 --> 00:12:45.830
katana-wielding vampire face off against a
blood-sucking megalomaniac who aims to unleash

00:12:45.830 --> 00:12:47.090
the "blood god."

00:12:47.090 --> 00:12:52.610
In Blade and company's quest to save humanity,
the gang tracks a suspicious human familiar

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:54.350
to a secret vampire club.

00:12:54.350 --> 00:13:00.389
There, they meet Pearl, whose delicate name
betrays the fact that this vampire is a grotesque

00:13:00.389 --> 00:13:01.626
heap of flesh.

00:13:01.626 --> 00:13:07.416
Pearl is a sight to behold — from his ferocious
flatulence to his childlike high-pitch voice.

00:13:07.416 --> 00:13:12.050
The latter aspect is especially disturbing
in light of director Stephen Norrington's

00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:17.550
statement on the DVD commentary that Pearl's
blob-like size is the result of his endless

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:19.211
hunger for infants.

00:13:19.211 --> 00:13:22.775
"And there's nothing you can do about it,
daywalker!"

00:13:22.775 --> 00:13:24.355
"Is that so?"

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:29.649
Somewhere deep beneath several inches of foam
latex is actor Eric Edwards.

00:13:29.649 --> 00:13:34.660
While Pearl took multiple crew members to
fully operate, it's Edwards who gets the credit

00:13:34.660 --> 00:13:37.889
for being entombed in 700 pounds of latex.

00:13:37.889 --> 00:13:42.830
Born in Kansas, Edwards' other on-screen performances
include supporting roles in "Sgt.

00:13:42.830 --> 00:13:44.780
Bilko" and "Candyman."

00:13:44.780 --> 00:13:49.600
Long before Godzilla became a digital CGI
goliath, actors portrayed the king of all

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:53.190
monsters by stomping around in heavy, sweaty
suits.

00:13:53.190 --> 00:13:58.160
Through forced perspective, human-sized costumes
appeared to be towering giants, capable of

00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:02.230
easily crushing cars, buildings, and historical
landmarks.

00:14:02.230 --> 00:14:08.380
Of all the actors tasked with bringing kaiju
to life, one name reigns supreme: Haruo Nakajima.

00:14:08.380 --> 00:14:14.820
Born in 1929, Nakajima began his film career
as a stuntman in samurai movies and war films,

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:17.699
including Akira Kurosawa's "Seven Samurai."

00:14:17.699 --> 00:14:22.410
Nakajima then portrayed Godzilla in twelve
consecutive films, making him one of the most

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:25.580
beloved and prolific "suit actors" in the
kaiju genre.

00:14:25.580 --> 00:14:31.800
In a 2017 video interview with Great Big Story,
Nakajima shared that he would observe animals

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:35.660
at the zoo on lunch breaks to find inspiration
for Godzilla's walk.

00:14:35.660 --> 00:14:40.080
He also explained that because rubber was
in short supply in Japan following World War

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:44.990
II, the suit for Godzilla was made out of
mixed concrete in the early days, making it

00:14:44.990 --> 00:14:46.300
extremely heavy.

00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:52.250
Nakajima retired from suit acting in 1972
and passed away from pneumonia complications

00:14:52.250 --> 00:14:53.250
in 2017.

00:14:53.250 --> 00:14:58.710
The filmmaking team behind 2019's "Godzilla:
King of the Monsters" dedicated the film to

00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:00.529
his memory.

00:15:00.529 --> 00:15:05.639
Despite being the Universal Monster with the
least amount of credits to his name, Gill-man

00:15:05.639 --> 00:15:08.850
is one of the most compelling creature designs
out there.

00:15:08.850 --> 00:15:14.279
Debuting in Jack Arnold's "Creature from the
Black Lagoon," Gill-man's design was, until

00:15:14.279 --> 00:15:19.070
recently, unsung handiwork by Disney animator
Milicent Patrick.

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:22.290
Gill-Man is the perfect balance of pathos
and practicality.

00:15:22.290 --> 00:15:27.279
Without room for words, it fell to the men
behind the airtight molded sponge rubber masks

00:15:27.279 --> 00:15:32.790
to humanize the creature's dexterous webbed
hands, powerful legs, and accusatory gaze.

00:15:32.790 --> 00:15:37.720
While Ben Chapman, who portrayed the Gill-Man
above water, certainly deserves recognition,

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:42.570
it's the other man behind the suit who has
inextricably linked himself with the Creature:

00:15:42.570 --> 00:15:43.620
Ricou Browning.

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:49.380
At 23, Browning worked at Weeki-Wachee Springs,
a freshwater roadside tourist attraction.

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:53.329
Some out-of-town Hollywood types asked him
to help with location scouting, and before

00:15:53.329 --> 00:15:57.956
long, they were asking him to portray the
titular creature in the film's underwater scenes.

00:15:57.956 --> 00:16:02.480
While Browning ultimately pivoted to working
behind the camera — as a writer, director,

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:08.110
and stunt coordinator — he reprised his
role as the underwater Gill-man for two sequels.

00:16:08.110 --> 00:16:12.589
Ever since Gaston Leroux published his horror
story "The Phantom of the Opera" in the early

00:16:12.589 --> 00:16:17.450
1900s, the story of Erik and Christine has
been adapted dozens of times.

00:16:17.450 --> 00:16:22.980
But of all the interpretations — from Broadway
musicals to "Phantom of the Paradise" to "Phantom

00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:28.480
of the Mall: Eric's Revenge" — it's the
Phantom of the 1925 film that haunts our dreams

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:31.459
most, played by the legendary Lon Chaney.

00:16:31.459 --> 00:16:36.910
Born on April Fool's Day 1883 to two deaf
parents, Chaney developed a reputation for

00:16:36.910 --> 00:16:39.050
being the "Man of 1,000 Faces."

00:16:39.050 --> 00:16:44.370
A self-taught makeup artist and a talented
thespian, Chaney's screen presence and transformative

00:16:44.370 --> 00:16:48.250
power reverberate through the decades with
undeniable clarity.

00:16:48.250 --> 00:16:53.839
The "uncanny valley" defines Chaney's Phantom:
his cavernous mouth, pit of a nose, engorged

00:16:53.839 --> 00:16:57.259
eyes, and sunken cheeks almost seem inhuman.

00:16:57.259 --> 00:17:00.380
When asked about his methods, Chaney told
American Cinematographer —

00:17:00.380 --> 00:17:06.486
"I'm supposed to have evolved some magic process
of malforming my features and limbs.

00:17:06.486 --> 00:17:08.176
It's an art, not magic."

00:17:08.280 --> 00:17:12.870
For the Phantom, Chaney contoured his face
and cheekbones, flattened his ears to his

00:17:12.870 --> 00:17:17.990
skull, wore an exaggerated bald cap that elevated
his forehead, and used wires to manipulate

00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:21.160
the curvature of his nostrils.

00:17:21.160 --> 00:17:25.549
Frankenstein's monster is one of the most
recognizable characters in horror history,

00:17:25.549 --> 00:17:30.210
and no version of the man-made man is more
iconic than the one in Universal Pictures'

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:31.590
series of films.

00:17:31.590 --> 00:17:37.490
In 1931, Universal's "Frankenstein" hit theaters,
featuring Colin Clive as the doctor and Boris

00:17:37.490 --> 00:17:39.430
Karloff as his monster.

00:17:39.430 --> 00:17:45.570
Born William Henry Pratt, Boris Karloff got
his start on the Canadian stage in the early 1910s.

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:50.890
He ultimately made his way to Hollywood, appearing
in silent films where his Anglo-Indian heritage

00:17:50.890 --> 00:17:54.750
led to castings in Arab, Indigenous, and Asian
roles.

00:17:54.750 --> 00:17:59.559
Karloff racked up eighty-one credits before
being cast in "Frankenstein" and its sequel,

00:17:59.559 --> 00:18:00.919
"Bride of Frankenstein."

00:18:00.919 --> 00:18:05.640
Jack Pierce, who had previously worked on
Universal Pictures' "Dracula," conceived the

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:07.710
makeup for Frankenstein's monster.

00:18:07.710 --> 00:18:12.470
Of all the details that make up the monster's
final appearance — the rectangular skull,

00:18:12.470 --> 00:18:16.550
the overhanging brow, the towering height
— one of the subtler features was something

00:18:16.550 --> 00:18:18.070
of a happy accident.

00:18:18.070 --> 00:18:22.940
As James L. Neibar relayed in "The Monster
Movies of Universal Studios," Karloff and

00:18:22.940 --> 00:18:27.799
Pierce achieved the creature's sunken cheeks
by removing the actor's dental plate.

